    "lawCode": "Add §390-e, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6088\n\nSPONSOR: LIU\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general business law, in relation to requiring a\nnotice to be posted regarding skimming\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo notify consumers and users of electronic benefit transfer cards of\npotential skimming risks.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the general business law by adding a new section 390-e\nrequiring a skimming awareness notice.\n\nSubdivision 1(a) defines \"electronic benefit transfer card.\"\n\nSubdivision 1(b) defines \"skimming.\"\n\nSubdivision 2 requires every person, firm, partnership, association or\ncorporation that is engaged in a sales transaction and accepts electron-\nic benefit transfer cards to place a sign at the point of sale contain-\ning a notice regarding skimming, the language of which shall be promul-\ngated by the secretary of state. States that said sign shall include\nsteps customers can take to protect themselves from skimming as well as\nan appropriate contact number to report incidences of suspected skim-\nming.\n\nSubdivision 3(a) imposes a civil penalty that shall not exceed two\nhundred fifty dollars per point of sale that lacks a skimming notice on\nany person, firm, partnership, association or corporation that fails to\ncomply with the requirements of this section. Subdivision 3(b) states\nthat should the provided sign be removed, damaged, or altered by any\nperson other than the person or entities required to provide such\nnotice, such person or entities shall have no liability for failure to\ncomply with subdivision 2 as long as such notice is replaced within ten\nbusiness days from becoming aware of the defect.\n\nSection 2 sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAccording to OTDA data provided to the Legal Aid Society of New York, at\nleast 2,208 low-income New Yorkers have been affected by benefit theft\nthrough EBT card skimming as of August 2022. This bill seeks to protect\npersonal consumer data by increasing consumers' and sellers' understand-\ning of skimming and requiring sellers or points of sales to notify\ncustomers of potential skimming schemes.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a New Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
